<p>A federal judge in Manhattan declined to dismiss US drug-trafficking and narcoterrorism charges against deposed Venezuelan leader Nicolás Maduro and his wife, Cilia Flores, after a hearing focused on whether US sanctions can block Venezuelan state funds from paying for their legal defense. Judge Alvin Hellerstein signaled that Maduro and Flores had not cleared the high bar for throwing out the indictment, but he also pressed the government on whether blocking payment for counsel of their choice could conflict with Sixth Amendment protections. The dispute has become a central procedural issue in the broader case, which began after US forces captured Maduro and Flores in Caracas in January and transferred them to New York. Both have pleaded not guilty and remain jailed in Brooklyn pending trial. Prosecutors said the sanctions serve national security and foreign policy interests and argued that Maduro had “plundered” Venezuela’s wealth, while the judge called the right to legal counsel “paramount,” suggesting the court may still revisit the funding question even as the criminal case moves forward.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/dbyPdjoN8OSB69GEbhd4wV8tafGuPN1Z6FWjrpiqgKAVBwvrtHJ_SvXEYjT3HR9Wpneqv7CCAMdoB8nFeODZ2USSqJAh2yAcj368iR_Nwagu' alt='Ousted Venezuelan President Nicolas Maduro with lawyer Barry Pollack at a narco-terrorism hearing in New York on March 26, shown in a courtroom sketch.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Court appearance: Dawn and El País said both defendants wore beige prison garb, while SCMP said Maduro appeared relaxed during the hearing.</li><li>Hearing length: Mercopress said the dispute over how the defense could be funded made the second hearing unusually long.</li><li>Isolation conditions: CBS reported that Maduro is being held in near-total isolation in a Brooklyn jail as the legal fight over representation and sanctions continues.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Judge Alvin Hellerstein: He questioned whether the government could keep blocking Venezuelan funds for the defense when Maduro and Flores were already in US custody and, in his view, no longer posed a national security threat. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1985654/us-judge-questions-block-on-maduros-use-of-venezuelan-funds-for-defence'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>US prosecutors: They argued that Maduro should not be allowed to use Venezuelan public money for his lawyers, saying the sanctions rest on legitimate national security and foreign policy grounds and portraying him as having taken Venezuela’s wealth for himself. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c4g8wxyq7v5o'>BBC</a>)</li><li>Maduro defense team: The defense argued that blocking access to Venezuelan state funds interferes with Maduro and Flores’s constitutional right to counsel of their choice because they cannot personally pay the legal bills. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/nicolas-maduros-lawyer-argues-against-u-s-blocking-funding-for-drug-trafficking-case-defense'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/nicolas-maduros-lawyer-argues-against-u-s-blocking-funding-for-drug-trafficking-case-defense'>Nicolás Maduro&#x27;s lawyer argues against U.S. blocking funding for drug trafficking case defense</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1985654/us-judge-questions-block-on-maduros-use-of-venezuelan-funds-for-defence'>US judge questions block on Maduro’s use of Venezuelan funds for defence</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/03/26/judge-refuses-to-dismiss-maduro-case-but-questions-blocking-of-funds-for-his-defense'>Judge refuses to dismiss Maduro case but questions blocking of funds for his defense</a> - mercopress.com</li><li><a href='https://english.elpais.com/usa/2026-03-26/judge-refuses-to-dismiss-narcoterrorism-case-against-nicolas-maduro-and-his-wife.html'>Judge refuses to dismiss narcoterrorism case against Nicolás Maduro and his wife</a> - el